1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,100
well, why they fail to create cohesive systems.

2
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:05,510
Right. They just end up completely fragmented.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:06,660
Exactly. Yeah.

4
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:10,670
And we're gonna explore why a national enterprise architecture, or EA,

5
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:12,750
is sort of the ultimate fix.

6
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:22,330
Our mission today is to equip you with the exact arguments your minister needs to understand why individual projects just can't solve systemic digital bloat.

7
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:24,810
Yeah. It is a crucial conversation to have.

8
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:32,510
So let's jump right in. In your ministry, you probably already require open APIs and interoperability in, like, every contract,

9
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:35,410
but the systems still remain disconnected.

10
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:40,210
Why are teams building a brand-new digital register instead of reusing an existing one?

11
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:42,390
I mean, is that just a failure of discipline?

12
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:45,870
Uh, no, actually. The team is acting completely rationally.

13
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:48,130
Wait, really? How is building from scratch rational?

14
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:50,690
Well, procurement rules do mandate behavior, sure.

15
00:00:50,719 --> 00:00:54,350
Mm. But they don't make that behavior the cheapest or, you know, the easiest choice.

16
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:55,570
Ah, I see what you mean.

17
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:55,870
Right.

18
00:00:56,710 --> 00:01:06,440
To a project team with a strict budget and a really tight deadline, reusing another ministry's system means learning it, negotiating access, and basically accepting

19
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:06,889
delays.

20
00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:08,230
So it just slows them down.

21
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:11,770
Exactly. Building their own building block is simply faster.

22
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:14,389
They are doing exactly what they were funded to do.

23
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:16,610
Okay, that makes sense on a micro level.

24
00:01:16,650 --> 00:01:20,470
But if the project teams are acting rationally within those constraints,

25
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:26,540
how do we show the minister that this is, you know, financially unsustainable across the government?

26
00:01:26,690 --> 00:01:31,290
So the math only really changes when you zoom out from that isolated project view.

27
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:32,610
Right. You need the bigger picture.

28
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:34,630
Exactly. Let's count out the whole of government view.

29
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:37,970
Ministry one pays to build the identity system.

30
00:01:38,090 --> 00:01:44,170
Okay. Then ministry two consumes it, ministry three consumes it, ministry four consumes it.

31
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:47,200
Wow, so you have four ministries running on that one initial investment.

32
00:01:47,410 --> 00:01:52,170
Precisely. Over five years, the country saves a massive share of its digital spend.

33
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:58,530
This portfolio level view is exactly what an EA provides your minister for funding decisions.

34
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:00,270
That is a huge financial win.

35
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:05,630
A minister will definitely grasp the cost savings of avoiding duplicate builds.

36
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:08,930
Oh, absolutely. But beyond just immediate cost savings,

37
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:14,710
what long-term necessities are these individual projects failing to deliver to your program?

38
00:02:14,910 --> 00:02:17,890
Well, there are two specific things projects cannot deliver.

39
00:02:17,990 --> 00:02:19,850
Uh, the first is sustainability.

40
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:21,110
Sustainability in what sense?

41
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:24,170
Like, projects are incentivized to ship, right?

42
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:33,850
They aren't funded to plan for 12 years later when the original vendor vanishes or, you know, the open source library forks, and literally no one fully understands the system

43
00:02:33,890 --> 00:02:34,350
anymore.

44
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:36,410
Oh yeah, the classic legacy software trap.

45
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:38,910
That makes total sense. So what's the second thing?

46
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:46,119
The second is complexity reduction. Project teams lack the authority to say no to feature requests from all the stakeholders they must please.

47
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:48,850
Right. They just have to keep saying yes to keep everyone happy.

48
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:55,410
Exactly. And five years later, those accumulated yeses make the system too complex to maintain or change.

49
00:02:55,470 --> 00:02:59,959
So given the inevitability of vendor changes and all this accumulating complexity,

50
00:03:00,619 --> 00:03:05,959
how do we avoid paying for the exact same lessons other countries have learned the hard way over the last 20 years?

51
00:03:06,270 --> 00:03:12,150
By using reference architectures, um, specifically like the PAERA framework.

52
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:17,390
PAERA. Right, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

53
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:23,910
It basically writes down decades of global learning regarding what actually survives technology change.

54
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:25,870
Oh, wow. That's incredibly useful.

55
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:31,210
Yeah. It really is. Within this framework, the four-part picture is the artifact, and planning is the function.

56
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:33,980
Got it. So when we present the final recommendation,

57
00:03:34,710 --> 00:03:40,360
how do we summarize the absolute necessity of separating the architecture from the individual project deliverables?

58
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:46,950
It boils down to this, um, reuse, sustainability, and complexity reduction don't come from projects.

59
00:03:47,010 --> 00:03:47,930
They come from planning.

60
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:49,170
Right. Planning is the key.

61
00:03:49,210 --> 00:03:56,140
Yes. That is why your minister must commission an enterprise architecture separately and deliberately with sustained funding.

62
00:03:56,250 --> 00:03:58,120
It has to be its own distinct priority.

63
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:02,550
Well, this has been an incredibly eye-opening look at how to tackle digital bloat.

64
00:04:02,930 --> 00:04:04,649
Glad to shed some light on it.

